/T (Strategic Overview: Southeast Asia entered Q4 2025 as a core geoeconomic hinge in the Indo-Pacific, shaped by intensifying great-power rivalry, supply-chain reconfiguration, and strategic resource concentration. Most states continue to hedge, balancing economic ties with China and the U.S. while leaning on ASEAN centrality, to preserve trade access, investment inflows, and policy autonomy. Growth remains supported by resilient private demand and ongoing public investment, but downside risks are increasingly driven by tariff uncertainty, trade fragmentation, maritime insecurity, and climate/disaster exposure.)
